d graph on state space $\\mathcal{S}_M = \\{(r, b) : r \\text{ odd mod } 2^M, \\, 0 \\le b \\le B\\}$ where $b$ is a discretized drift budget tracking cumulative advantage above start.\n- **Edge condition:** $(r,b) \\to (r', b')$ when $r \\mapsto r'$ under $K$-step acceleration and $b' = \\min(b + \\lceil d(r) \\rceil, B)$ with $d(r) = K\\log_2 3 - V$ (net drift).\n- **Exit states:** When $b' < 0$, the orbit has fallen below starting value.\n\n#### **Computational verification** (Table 15190–15225, lines 15190–15238):\n- **Moduli tested:** $M = 6$ through $19$; block sizes $K = 1$ (single-step) and $K = 5$ (aggregated).\n- **Three-zone structure:**\n  - **Zone I** ($M = 6$–$9$): $G_M$ is acyclic. Ranking certificates exist.\n  - **Zone II** ($M = 10$–$12$): $G_M$ contains net-positive-drift cycles. The LP is infeasible; no ranking function exists.\n  - **Zone III** ($M \\ge 13$, verified through $19$): $G_M$ is acyclic again. Ranking certificates exist with $\\max V_M$ growing linearly in $M$.\n- **Cycle state counts:** Identical for $K=1$ and $K=5$, confirming intrinsic structure.\n\n#### **Carry parity obstruction** (Definition & Proposition, lines 15304–15361):\n\n**Exact statement (lines 15310–15316):**\n> \"A cycle $\\gamma$ at modular depth $M$ lifts to a cycle at depth $M+1$ only if $\\pi(\\gamma) = 0$. For every net-positive-drift cycle at $M = 10, 11,$ or $12$, the carry parity is $\\pi(\\gamma) = 1$ (odd).\"\n\n**What is it:** The carry parity $\\pi(\\gamma) = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} c_i \\bmod 2$ counts the parity of carries $c_i = \\lfloor T(r_i)/2^M \\rfloor \\bmod 2$ (whether the $(M+1)$-th bit is set at position $i$ around a cycle).\n\n**Status:** Computational verification shows every net-positive cycle at $M \\in \\{10,11,12\\}$ has $\\pi(\\gamma) = 1$. The lift fails because the carry chain has odd winding number and cannot close (lines 15318–15349).\n\n**Exact computational evidence (Table 15330–15341):**\n| $M$ | Cycle | $n$ | Carry=0 | Carry=1 | Parity |\n|-----|-------|-----|---------|---------|--------|\n| 10  | 1     | 26  | 25      | 1       | odd    |\n| 11  | 1     | 25  | 24      | 1       | odd    |\n| 12  | 1     | 7   | 6       | 1       | odd    |\n| 12  | 2     | 6   | 5       | 1       | odd    |\n\n**Connection to your parity channel:** The authors' \"carry parity obstruction\" is **not identical** to your v₂(3^ℓm−1) odd-parity bias requirement. Rather:\n- **Their result:** Modular cycles cannot lift because of topological winding constraints on the carry bits.\n- **Your result:** Ascending orbits need odd-parity v₂ values to sustain valuation gain.\n\nThese address *different failure modes*: theirs bars cycle *persistence across modular refinement*; yours concerns valuation *sustainability*. However, both are structural parities obstructing divergence. Your parity channel may be the arithmetic analog of their topological carry obstruction.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Net-Positive Ranking Lemma, Non-liftability, Birth Obstruction: Statements & Status**\n\n#### **Net-Positive Ranking Lemma** (lines 15469–15483, Lemma 15473):\n**Statement:**\n> \"For $M \\ge 13$ (verified through $M=19$), the net-positive state graph $G_M$ is acyclic. The function $V_M(s) = $ length of longest directed path from $s$ to an exit state satisfies $V_M(s') \\le V_M(s) - 1$ for every edge $s \\to s'$ in $G_M$.\"\n\n**Status:** Proven by acyclicity verification for $M=13$–$19$. Yields bounded above-start persistence: no trajectory can remain in the net-positive region for more than $\\max_s V_M(s)$ steps (empirically $\\approx 5M$ for $K=1$, $\\approx 2M$ for $K=5$).\n\n**Important caveat (Remark 15499–15516, \"Layer 1 vs Layer 2\"):** This is **Layer 1** (single excursion bound). **Layer 2** asks whether an orbit can repeatedly exit, wander through negative-drift states, and re-enter with restored budget. This is addressed by the augmented return graph $G_M^+$.\n\n#### **Non-liftability of net-positive cycles** (Proposition 15267–15284, lines 15267–15274):\n**Statement:**\n> \"Across all parameter pairs $(M,K)$ tested ($M=6$–$18$, $K=1$–$8$; 104 pairs total), net-positive-drift cycles exist only at $M=10, 11, 12$, and every such cycle has $D(M,K) = 0$: it fails to lift even one level.\"\n\n**Status:** Computational verification; all attempts to lift Zone II cycles to mod $2^{M+1}$ fail—either the cycle fails to close or acquires net-negative drift.\n\n#### **Cycle genealogy and birth obstruction** (Proposition 15434–15447, lines 15434–15447):\n**Statement:**\n1. Net-positive cycles are born at $M=10, 11, 12$ and die at $M=11, 12, 13$ respectively (via carry parity obstruction).\n2. **No net-positive cycle is born at any $M \\ge 13$.**\n3. Only cycle at $M \\ge 13$ (verified through $18$) is trivial fixed point $r=1$ with $v_2=2$ and negative drift.\n\n**Status:** Computational genealogy tracking. The \"birth obstruction\" is the structural fact that positive-drift subgraph is acyclic (Prop. 15452–15459): max chain length equals exactly $M$, so any net-positive cycle requires detours through negative-drift residues, which the carry parity blocks upon refinement.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Fiber-57 structural programme: Definitions, theorems, explicit constants**\n\n**Location:** Lines 15791–16372 (Section: \"Fiber-57 structural programme\")\n\nFiber 57 = residue class $n \\equiv 57 \\pmod{64}$ (the sustaining set is quotient digits $q \\in \\{0,3,7\\} \\pmod{8}$).\n\n#### **Pair-return automaton & Exact return structure:**\n\n**Proposition (q≡7 two-step return, lines 15912–15945):**\n- For $n = 64q + 57$ with $q = 8m + 7$:\n  - $S(n) = 384m + 379 \\equiv 59 \\pmod{64}$ (exits fiber 57)\n  - $S^2(n) = 576m + 569 = 64(9m+8) + 57$ (returns to fiber 57 in **exactly 2 steps**)\n  - Destination quotient: $q' = 9m + 8 \\equiv m \\pmod{8}$ (uniform when $m$ is uniform)\n  - **Chain map:** Acts as left-shift in base 8; replaces $r$ low digits after $r$ consecutive returns.\n\n**Proposition (q≡3 minimum return 